From 783dfd21bc503a61a889ed4b62bfc71ebb92a77d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shibco <cade@parare.al>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 18:21:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] converge on a foreign quantum: predict, adopt, mute

Issue #49 (ableton-linux): a foreign forced quantum (global
clock.force-quantum from pipewire-jack, or another client's
node.force-quantum winning last-change arbitration) leaves the driver
feeding buffer_size frames per quantum-sized cycle: wrong-speed playback
with periodic waveform steps.

Predict: track the settings metadata's global clock.force-quantum;
GetBufferSize, Init and buffer sizing offer it so the host builds
matching buffers from the start. Every advertised quantum is validated
against the accepted range first, and the running clock.duration wins
over the raw metadata value (the daemon clamps forced values to
clock.quantum-limit, so metadata and reality can disagree); a size
CreateBuffers would reject is never advertised.

Adopt: the config watcher withdraws our node.force-quantum after two
consecutive mismatched polls and asks the host to rebuild at the
observed quantum, at most 3 adoptions a minute. The rebuild target is
committed only when the host's CreateBuffers actually arrives, so an
ignored reset can retry the same quantum; GetBufferSize returns the
target as preferred in host-controlled mode too, so a host that honours
kAsioResetRequest converges. A graph-wide quantum predicted before
CreateBuffers is committed on successful creation even without a reset
target. The adopted state survives ordinary Stop/Start and
DisposeBuffers/CreateBuffers cycles, and clears only when the forcing
condition ends (forcer node departs, global clock.force-quantum cleared)
or the user changes quantum policy; the registry tracks foreign
node.force-quantum carriers for that.

Mute: while sizes disagree the process path swaps silence both ways, so
the audible fault is silence plus one log line per episode. The host
callback is not run once per graph cycle at host size (a 512-frame host
on a 128-frame graph would burn 4x DSP): graph frames accumulate and
host-size-equivalent callbacks fire at the correct wall-clock cadence,
with a bounded loop when the quantum exceeds the host size, so the
transport advances at real speed and DSP stays at 1x.

PIPEASIO_ALLOW_QUANTUM_MISMATCH=on restores warn-and-play-on. Both
halves parse it (and every shared boolean variable) with one strict
case-insensitive helper, pipeasio_env_bool. It accepts 1/0,
true/false, yes/no, on/off and enabled/disabled; the old
lstrcmpiA-vs-strcmp split made '=ON' mute one half only.

Port buffers range up to the accepted maximum so a larger foreign
quantum always has a buffer to land in. The WoW64 proxy gains the four
calls (PAU_GLOBAL_FORCE_QUANTUM/QUANTUM_MUTED/FORCER_PRESENT/
DROP_FORCE_QUANTUM) and the ABI layout guard is extended to cover them.
